      "text": "1. Start from the given functions: \\[f = q \\circ p \\circ r.\\]\n2. First, recall that the inverse of a composition of functions can be handled step by step. Let $a = p(r(x))$, and we need to find the inverse of $f(p(r(x)))$.\n3. According to the definitions:\n   - The steps are:\n     \\[\n     q \\circ p \\circ r = q(p(r(x)))\n     \\]\n   - To find $f(x)^{-1}$, we need to reverse the order of the compositions and apply the inverses accordingly:\n     \\[\n     f(x)^{-1} = q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ r^{-1}\n     \\]\n4. Re-checking with the original truth, given:\n   - \\[\n   f = q \\circ p \\circ r\n   \\]\n   - Consider \\(a = p(r(x))\\):\n     \\[\n     f(p(r(x))) = q(p(r(x)))\n     \\]\n   - To find the inverse, we first reverse the order of the functions' inversions:\n     \\[\n     f^{-1}(x) = q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ r^{-1}(x)\n     \\]\n\nConclusion:\nThe inverse function for $f$ is:\n\\[ \\boxed{D} \\]",
