    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the inverse function \\( f^{-1} \\) for the function \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we will apply the rule of function inverses, which states that the inverse of a composition of functions is the composition of the inverses in reverse order. \n\nGiven \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we can express the inverse function \\( f^{-1} \\) as follows:\n\nSince \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), we have:\n\\[ f(x) = q(p(r(x))) \\]\n\nTo find the inverse, we start by setting \\( y = f(x) \\):\n\\[ y = q(p(r(x))) \\]\n\nNext, we need to isolate \\( x \\) in terms of \\( y \\). To do this, we will use the inverse functions step by step:\n\n1. First, apply the inverse of \\( q \\) to both sides:\n\\[ q^{-1}(y) = p(r(x)) \\]\n\n2. Next, apply the inverse of \\( p \\) to both sides:\n\\[ p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y)) = r(x) \\]\n\n3. Finally, apply the inverse of \\( r \\) to both sides:\n\\[ r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y))) = x \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ x = r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(y))) \\]\n\nTherefore, the inverse function \\( f^{-1} \\) can be expressed as:\n\\[ f^{-1}(x) = r^{-1}(p^{-1}(q^{-1}(x))) \\]\n\nThis matches option C:\n\\[ r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\]\n\nSo the correct answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{C} \\]",
